{
	title: 'Terminal blocks link to their web card',
	description: "Every block the terminal draws with a header (prompt, tool call, question, error; as in the old Hal's buildHeader, which showed a short ref at the right of the header, e.g. 04-fyx/0gdec4) carries an OSC 8 link to the same block on the web: <webUrl>/<session id>#<block id>, the id that task 0z uses, with the hidden ?auth= link code of task e3. The link sits on the header's ref or time text; the visible text shows no code. tasks/README.md's rule: anything linkable in one client is linkable in the other. Tests: a tool block's header links to the address the web resolves to that card; the visible row shows no code; a block without an id (streaming before its first byte has one) is not linked.",
	status: 'done',
	needs: ['0z', 'e3'],
	foldInto: ['0z'],
	notes: [
		"hal2's terminal draws no header rows, ref or time (items start with a marker: '> ', '▸ ', '? ', 'error:'), so the link sits on what stands in for the header: a tool call's whole row (the terminal shows a glimpse, the card all of it), else the leading marker token. No visible ref was added: it would steal columns from wrapped prompt text. itemView.linked does it after itemLines; frame.itemRows takes the session id (transcript.meta.id) and caches per session and key.",
		'ansi.webUrl puts ?auth= before a #fragment: appended after it, the code would land in the hash and the page would get neither the code nor the block id.',
		'The block-id rule (digits, optional .<i>; `~` keys are not linkable) lives in common transcript.href, shared by web target.ts and the terminal. The frame test resolves terminal links with web target.parse (tests may cross layers).',
		'Tool results, text, thinking and command output are not linked; a result links through its call row, which the web maps to the same card.'
	]
}
